---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Rendering">
  <p>
    ArrowCrab renders everything on an HTML5 Canvas 2D context. There's no DOM
    for graph elements; every node, edge, port, and label is a direct canvas
    draw call.
  </p>

  <h2>Coordinate System</h2>
  <p>
    The layout algorithm works in <strong>grid units</strong> (GU). One grid
    unit is 12 pixels (<code>GRID_UNIT = 12</code>). The viewport applies
    translation and scale via <code>context.translate()</code> /
    <code>context.scale()</code>, so drawing code doesn't deal with screen
    coordinates.
  </p>
  <p>
    This keeps layout math in small integers while the viewport handles
    pixel-level precision for smooth pan and zoom.
  </p>

  <h2>Scene Object</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>GraphScene</code> holds everything for rendering and interaction:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Canvas 2D context</li>
    <li>Graph model (nodes, scopes, adjacency)</li>
    <li>Layout map (grid-unit positions, spatial index per scope)</li>
    <li>Routing map (pixel waypoints per scope)</li>
    <li>Expanded-node set (which compound nodes are open)</li>
    <li>Viewport state (offset, scale)</li>
    <li>Hit regions (toggle buttons, leaf nodes, expanded nodes, edges)</li>
    <li>Selection and highlight sets</li>
    <li>
      Cached global adjacency (lazily built, invalidated on expansion changes)
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Viewport Culling</h2>
  <p>
    Before drawing, <code>render()</code> figures out which rectangle is visible
    in graph-pixel coordinates. Each scope's spatial index (a grid-hash over
    node bounding boxes, built once during layout) returns only the nodes that
    overlap the visible area. Nodes outside the viewport don't get drawn, don't
    get hit regions, and don't touch the canvas API.
  </p>
  <p>
    Edges are culled the same way: the bounding box of each edge's waypoints
    is checked against the visible rectangle.
  </p>
  <p>
    This cuts per-frame work from O(N) to O(visible nodes). At typical zoom
    levels only a few hundred of potentially thousands of nodes are on screen.
  </p>

  <h2>Level of Detail</h2>
  <p>
    Visible nodes are drawn at a detail level based on their screen-pixel size:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Screen width</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td>&gt; 40 px</td><td>Full: body, stroke, label, ID, toggle, ports</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>8 to 40 px</td><td
          >Simplified: filled rounded rect, stroke, no text or ports</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr><td>2 to 8 px</td><td>Dot: filled rectangle only</td></tr>
      <tr><td>&lt; 2 px</td><td>Invisible: skipped entirely</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Text rendering (especially <code>context.font</code> changes) is the most
    expensive part of drawing. LOD skips it at zoom levels where you couldn't
    read the text anyway.
  </p>

  <h2>Render Order</h2>
  <p>Within each scope, rendering follows a fixed order:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Edges first.</strong> Routed polylines are drawn before nodes so that
      nodes visually sit on top.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nodes.</strong> For each node in the scope:
      <ul>
        <li>
          Collapsed compound nodes and leaf nodes go through <code
            >drawNode</code
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          Expanded compound nodes render their background, label bar, ports,
          then recursively render child subgraphs.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    This way child content always paints on top of parent backgrounds, and edges
    never cover node bodies.
  </p>

  <h2>Node Drawing</h2>
  <p>
    Each node is a rounded rectangle filled with its color and stroked with a
    darkened variant. On top of that:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Label</strong> - centered white text with a black stroke outline
      so it's readable on any background.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>ID badge</strong> - small monospace number outside the top-right
      corner, for debugging.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Toggle button</strong> - <code>[+]</code>/<code
        >[&minus;]</code
      > in the top-left of compound nodes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ports</strong> - small circles on the top (inputs) and bottom
      (outputs) edges, numbered.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Ports draw last so they paint over the node border.
  </p>

  <h2>Edge Drawing</h2>
  <p>
    Edges are orthogonal polylines with rounded corners. <code>smoothPath()</code>
    inserts quadratic B&eacute;zier curves at each waypoint to round the
    90&deg; turns. Each edge ends with a triangular arrowhead.
  </p>

  <h3>Edge Colors</h3>
  <p>
    Edges use a <strong>golden-angle palette</strong>. Each edge index maps to
    a hue:
  </p>
  <pre><code>hue = (0.58 + index * 0.618...) mod 1</code></pre>
  <p>
    The golden angle spaces successive colors as far apart as possible in hue
    before any repeat. Saturation is low (18%) and lightness moderate (58%) so
    the colors look muted and readable on the <code>#141414</code> background.
  </p>

  <h3>Crossing Markers</h3>
  <p>
    Where two edges cross, a small hollow circle is drawn so you can tell
    whether paths are connected (they share a node) or just crossing (they share
    a pixel).
  </p>

  <h2>Subgraph Frames</h2>
  <p>
    Expanded compound nodes show child subgraphs inside bordered frames. Each
    frame is a rounded rectangle filled with <code>#141414</code> and stroked
    with a darkened version of the parent node's color. Multiple subgraphs in
    the same parent are separated by a thin vertical divider.
  </p>
  <p>
    Boundary ports (entry/exit points) are circles on the top and bottom edges
    of the frame, same style as regular node ports.
  </p>

  <h2>Text Rendering</h2>
  <p>
    All text goes through <code>outlinedText()</code>, which strokes behind the
    fill (like SVG's <code>paint-order: stroke fill</code>) so labels are
    readable on any node color.
  </p>
  <p>
    To avoid redundant <code>context.font</code> assignments (one of the most
    expensive Canvas 2D state changes), the current font, alignment, and baseline
    are tracked at module level. Canvas state only updates when it actually
    changes.
  </p>

  <h2>Visual Theme</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Element</th><th>Value</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Background</td><td><code>#141414</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Text (primary)</td><td><code>#ffffff</code></td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td>Text (secondary)</td><td
          ><code>#cccccc</code> / <code>#dddddd</code></td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr><td>Node body</td><td>From JSON data (arbitrary CSS color)</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Node stroke</td><td>30% darkened variant of body color</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Port fill</td><td><code>#1c2030</code></td></tr>
      <tr
        ><td>Subgraph frame</td><td
          ><code>#141414</code> fill, 10% darkened parent stroke</td
        ></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td>Fonts</td><td
          >System sans-serif for labels, monospace for IDs and ports</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>
</DocsLayout>
